"""fleet-nightly - every night, the dev-stack tests and the Quickstarts on each clean test VM, one VM after another,
from fresh clones of main; a machine-readable result for a status page, never an alert.

    tools/fleet-nightly [--config FILE] [--os debian,freebsd,...] [--legs dev-docker,...] [--budget MINUTES]
                        [--out DIR] [--keep-src] [--no-publish] [--dry-run]

One run:

 1. takes the run lock (OUT/.run.lock): a second run started while one is going exits at once (status 0);
 2. clones machiya, kura, niwa and konbini fresh from main (`git_url`, `{repo}` filled in) and notes each commit;
 3. for each OS in `order`: takes that VM's lock (`lock_dir`/testvm-<os>.lock, the one the agents take with
    flock(1)) WITHOUT waiting: if another run holds it, the OS is skipped and the summary says so. Then for each leg:
    reverts the VM (`vmctl revert <vm>`: a fresh overlay, booted), waits for a privileged login, runs the leg,
    and records pass/fail, the duration and a short excerpt of the output. After the last leg it stops the VM
    (`vmctl stop <vm>`) and releases the lock. Every leg starts from a revert, so no leg sees another's packages;
 4. writes OUT/nightly.json and OUT/nightly.txt (and a copy of both plus every leg's full log in OUT/runs/<stamp>/,
    the last `keep_runs` kept), deletes the clones, and pipes nightly.json to `publish` (a shell command), if set.

Legs (each OS lists its own in `oses.<os>.legs`):

  dev-docker         machiya tools/dev-test VM --packages                      (Linux: the stack in Docker)
  dev-native         machiya tools/dev-test VM --packages --native  + --hister-bin FILE (`hister_bin`) or
                     --hister-via <the Hister host> (a kept Docker dev stack on `hister_host`, started on first
                     need, kept until the last OS that needs it, then stopped)
  stack-quickstart   machiya tools/quickstart-test --with-packages, on the VM  (the README's stack Quickstart)
  kura-native        kura tools/quickstart-test --only native --ssh VM
  kura-container     kura tools/quickstart-test --only container --ssh VM      (podman)
  niwa-native        niwa tools/quickstart-test --only native --with-packages, on the VM
  niwa-container     niwa tools/quickstart-test --only container --with-packages --engine docker, on the VM
  konbini-native     konbini tools/quickstart-test --only native --with-packages, on the VM
  konbini-container  konbini tools/quickstart-test --only container --with-packages, on the VM (podman)

"On the VM" legs copy the four clones (with .git) to `remote_dir` there and need python3 and git first: the
`bootstrap` line of the OS installs just those two (the README's own package block then does the rest).

A total time budget (`budget_minutes`) bounds the run: a leg never runs past it (its timeout is cut to what is
left) and no leg starts once it is spent; those legs are `skipped` with the reason. A leg is pass, fail, timeout,
error (the VM, the copy or the bootstrap failed: not the software under test) or skipped.

Exit status: 0 when every leg that ran passed, 1 when one failed, 2 when the runner itself could not run (no
clones, no `vmctl`). Settings: built-in defaults < the JSON config file (--config, $FLEET_NIGHTLY_CONFIG, or
~/.config/machiya/fleet-nightly.json) < $FLEET_VMCTL, $FLEET_GIT_URL, $FLEET_NIGHTLY_DIR, $FLEET_NIGHTLY_PUBLISH <
the options. docs/dev-stack.md ("Nightly") has the JSON shape, the timer and the install steps. Nothing secret is
printed or written to the summary: the stacks make their own dummy secrets, and excerpts are scrubbed of anything
that looks like a token. Standard library only; needs git, ssh and tar here.
"""
